 that  even with  diet culture being overwhelmingly  pervasive  we can slowly start to change the way we  talk  and eventually think about different  foods  but anyways this does sound like a  really great lunch we've got lots of  healthy fats in the salmon  and fiber with the kale i mean we could  definitely  add a little carb in there maybe some  bread on the side or some couscous or  quinoa  in there but judging by lucy's next  lunch pick  it doesn't sound like she's anti-carb a  club sandwich  i made one the other day you butter two  sides  of wheat toast and you brown them and  then in another pan you fry an egg  and on the fried egg you put vegan  cheddar cheese i  grilled up some turkey bacon a  nitrate-free turkey breast  smack that uh yes  this is ticking all the right boxes here  so  we've got protein in the turkey and egg  healthy fats in the avocado  carbs in the bread and lots of veggies  going on  but let's talk for a second about  nitrate free  so nitrates have become a bit of a bad  word in health circles  since we do have research to suggest  that heating up nitrates in foods like  meat  create carcinogenous compounds called  nitrosamines  but are deli meats that are labeled  nitrate-free  actually nitrate-free well in short  not exactly food manufacturers still  cure their nitrate-free bacon or turkey  breast for example  but they rely on natural curing agents  like celery powder  in lieu of synthetic sodium nitrate and  while we don't have any quality research  to suggest that  natural nitrates are healthier than  synthetic ones  most experts think that the effect is  likely the same  and actually there is some concern that  you might actually be getting  more nitrates in nitrate-free foods  since synthetic nitrate amounts are  regulated and capped whereas the amount  of celery powder a food can contain  is not having said that i don't think  that we need to sensationalize or  demonize anything here there are  nitrates in all sorts of the foods that  we consume most notably actually  vegetables and if you're consuming  processed foods in moderation it  shouldn't be a huge concern  so i say that there's totally room for  moderate amounts of processed foods if  you enjoy  them when the bulk of your diet is  coming from whole  unprocessed foods overall diet quality  is  much more important to me than a singled  out ingredient  when it comes to achieving long-term  good health  favorite food city ooh i recently went  to barcelona  this was the weirdest thing i ate in  barcelona peeled  white asparagus mixed with sea urchin  uni  but it was interesting you know i think  that's the coolest thing about travel is  tasting other cultures foods  i 100 agree on both friends  barcelona is a beautiful food city  though i think if we're talking spain  i'm partial to san sebastian but let me  know  below your all-time favorite food city  so usually what i do at work i'll  postmate something  i'm a big soup eater i solely survived  on wonton soup chicken wonton soup  and faux vegetarian faux i think soup  is the ultimate example of why comfort  food  doesn't always need to mean junk food  i mean soup to me is inherently  comforting  especially for us canadians in the dead  of winter  yet it's also a natural base for  building a  super balanced bowl it is also insanely  delicious so that gets huge thumbs up  from me  [Applause]  weekend cheat meal lunch well i usually  if i'm going to cheat  i go cheat meal dinner and  my favorite shoestring french fries  with a buttermilk ranch dressing and  like a  really good burger  oh lucy you failed my cheat meal test  but at least i am with you on loving a  good burger and fries  i am also kind of surprised that she's a  diet coke girl especially  with her cheat meal i always call this  out as like the diet  coke effect you know when people feel  good that they're choosing the  calorie-free diet soda so that  they give themselves permission to go  ham on the burger and fries  now i'm 